💭 test: unoptimized leak_check
💭 A same-class error-prone generic initializer that directly raises must not leak the dynamically allocated generic
💭 type description it stored on the receiver before failing (#133). A purely inherited initializer, reached by
💭 directly instantiating a subclass that declares neither fields nor an initializer of its own, must not leak it
💭 either: the receiver's runtime class info then never matches the initializer's own owner, so the caller that
💭 requested the object, not the initializer itself, owns the description. A statically known description (no
💭 dynamic generic argument) must never be passed to free(), and an initializer that fails after a successful
💭 explicit superinitializer call must free the description exactly once, from the frame that owns it.

🐇 🐠🐚T⚪🍆 🍇
  🖍🆕 value T

  🆕 v T fail 👌 🚧🚧 🍇
    ↪️ fail 🍇 🚨🆕🚧 🔤failed🔤❗️ 🍉
    v ➡️ 🖍value
  🍉

  ❗️ 🥇 ➡️ T 🍇
    ↩️ value
  🍉
🍉

💭 🐟 declares neither fields nor an initializer, and directly inherits 🐠's error-prone initializer.
🐇 🐟🐚T⚪🍆 🐠🐚T🍆 🍇🍉

💭 🦐 does the same from 🐟, one level further, without declaring an initializer of its own either.
🐇 🦐🐚T⚪🍆 🐟🐚T🍆 🍇🍉

💭 💰's deinitializer is a sentinel: it must fire exactly once whenever a 💰 instance is actually released (either
💭 as a field of a failed initializer being cleaned up, or as part of a successfully constructed object's own
💭 destruction), and never as a side effect of freeing a type description.
🐇 💰 🍇
  🖍🆕 n 🔢

  🆕 🍇
    0 ➡️ 🖍n
  🍉

  ♻️ 🍇
    😀 🔤💰 deinit🔤❗️
  🍉
🍉

💭 🦑 declares its own field and explicitly calls 🐠's initializer, so the description it received from its caller
💭 must be freed exactly once, by 🦑's own frame, if 🦑 raises after a successful super call. Its own deinitializer
💭 sentinel must never fire when 🦑 (or a subclass of it) fails to initialize, only on normal destruction.
🐇 🦑🐚T⚪🍆 🐠🐚T🍆 🍇
  🖍🆕 resource 💰

  🆕 v T raise 👌 🚧🚧 🍇
    🆕💰❗️ ➡️ 🖍resource
    ⤴️🆕 v 👎❗️
    ↪️ raise 🍇 🚨🆕🚧 🔤failed🔤❗️ 🍉
  🍉

  ♻️ 🍇
    😀 🔤🦑 deinit🔤❗️
  🍉
🍉

💭 🐳 extends 🦑 one level further, forming a multi-level explicit-super chain (🐳 ➡️ 🦑 ➡️ 🐠), and can fail before,
💭 during, or after its own explicit superinitializer call. Its own field (🖍extra) and deinitializer sentinel let
💭 execution confirm 🐳's own field is released on every failure path and that 🐳's own deinitializer never runs for
💭 a partially initialized object. Description ownership itself (freed exactly once regardless of which frame in the
💭 chain observes the failure, at both optimized and unoptimized settings) is verified independently by the
💭 EMOJICODE_CHECK_DESCRIPTION_LEAKS execution check (see leak_check_tests in tests.py), not by this output. Inherited
💭 fields initialized by a successful super call are released exactly once by the failing frame (#126): in the
💭 "during" case, 🦑's frame releases its resource and 🐠's value; in the "after" case, 🐳's frame releases both, so
💭 each prints one 💰 deinit before the error plus one for v when 🦈 releases its argument afterwards.
🐇 🐳🐚T⚪🍆 🦑🐚T🍆 🍇
  🖍🆕 extra 💰

  🆕 v T before 👌 duringFail 👌 after 👌 🚧🚧 🍇
    ↪️ before 🍇 🚨🆕🚧 🔤failed before super🔤❗️ 🍉
    🆕💰❗️ ➡️ 🖍extra
    ⤴️🆕 v duringFail❗️
    ↪️ after 🍇 🚨🆕🚧 🔤failed after super🔤❗️ 🍉
  🍉

  ♻️ 🍇
    😀 🔤🐳 deinit🔤❗️
  🍉
🍉

